--TEST--
Core\Arr::reverse renumbers every key its preserveKeys option discards, where array_reverse renumbers the integers and keeps the strings
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
The same divergence `arr-slice-on-string-keys-diverges-from-array_slice` records, in the other member that carries the option: `rule:types/preserve-keys` removes every rule that asks what type a key is, and `array_reverse`'s `$preserve_keys = false` is one — it renumbers the integer keys and keeps the string ones, so the same call answers a list or a map depending on what the subject's keys happened to be. Novis's `{preserveKeys: false}` discards every key alike and the result is a list from "0", whatever went in. `{preserveKeys: true}` keeps every key alike in both languages, and that half of the option agrees entry for entry — which is what makes this a divergence about the meaning of `false` and not about the order the member produces.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<int> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// No integer key at all: `array_reverse($map)` answers `z=3;y=2;x=1;`, keeping
// all three strings even though the option asked for them to be dropped, where
// Novis numbers the reversed entries from zero.
array<int> $map = ["x" => 1, "y" => 2, "z" => 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::reverse($map)), "\n";

// Both kinds of key in one subject: `array_reverse($mixed)` answers
// `0=3;x=2;1=1;`, renumbering the two integer keys in their *new* order around
// a string it left where it was — so an entry's key after the call depends on
// how many entries of the other kind came before it.
array<int> $mixed = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::reverse($mixed)), "\n";

// The agreeing half, on the same subjects: asked to keep the keys, both
// languages keep all of them, so nothing about the order is in dispute.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::reverse($map, {preserveKeys: true})), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::reverse($mixed, {preserveKeys: true})), "\n";

// And the list case, where the two rules for `false` coincide because there is
// no string key for PHP to keep — asserted against PHP itself in
// `arr-reverse-matches-array_reverse`.
array<int> $list = [1, 2, 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::reverse($list)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0=3;1=2;2=1;
0=3;1=2;2=1;
z=3;y=2;x=1;
9=3;x=2;5=1;
0=3;1=2;2=1;
